WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.510
Registering step by step, and setting up properly.

2
00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:09.640
This is the video for module two of Business Registration and Compliance in Nigeria.

3
00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:20.700
You can register a business name yourself on the CAC portal, from cac.gov.ng. Screens change, so follow the labels you see on the day.

4
00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:25.790
Step one. Create your account with your own email and phone, never an agent's.

5
00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:31.840
Step two. Check your first name option. If it is refused, try the next one.

6
00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:40.490
Step three. Fill in the address, nature of business, and each owner's details, then upload a clear photo and signature.

7
00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:48.630
Describe the business in plain words, like production, packaging and sale of snacks. General business can cause a query.

8
00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:56.430
Step four. Review every spelling before you pay. Errors after approval are harder and costlier to fix.

9
00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:00.300
Use the name on your NIN, not a nickname.

10
00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:06.860
Step five. Pay on the portal and keep the payment reference. Screenshot each stage.

11
00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:14.660
Step six. Watch your email and dashboard, answer any query, and download your certificate when approved.

12
00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:20.800
If approved, download your documents immediately and save them in two places.

13
00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:32.010
If queried, fix exactly what is asked. For example, Kemi in Abeokuta was told her signature was unclear and her surname did not match her NIN.

14
00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:41.510
She re-signed in black pen on white paper, corrected her surname letter for letter, resubmitted, and was approved two days later without paying again.

15
00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:46.200
If your name is rejected, simply try your next option.

16
00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:56.850
A company needs more decisions: name ending in Limited, address, share capital, shareholders, directors, and people with significant control.

17
00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:02.920
For example, Chioma and her sister Adaeze in Enugu are registering a catering company.

18
00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:14.050
Chioma runs it full time and puts in 700,000 naira, so she takes 700,000 shares of 1 naira each. That is 70 percent.

19
00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:21.400
Adaeze puts in 300,000 naira and takes 300,000 shares, 30 percent.

20
00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:32.380
Their issued share capital is 1,000,000 naira, matching what they put in. Stamp duty is charged on share capital, so choose a figure you can justify.

21
00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:38.320
Both are directors, and they sign a short private agreement on what happens if one sister leaves.

22
00:02:39.570 --> 00:02:51.300
Many owners use an accredited agent for a company. Ask for their accreditation number and check it on the CAC website. Insist the portal account and email are yours.

23
00:02:52.550 --> 00:02:58.840
Ask for the CAC fee and their fee separately, in writing, and for every document after approval.

24
00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:09.110
And never let someone register the company in their name on your behalf. The person on the register is the legal owner. Your name, your NIN, your shares.

25
00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:17.640
The certificate is not the finish line. Read every line of your documents, and raise any error with the CAC quickly.

26
00:03:18.890 --> 00:03:26.250
Search your business on the CAC public search. Use it to check suppliers and agents too, before you send them money.

27
00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:39.770
Confirm your Tax ID status on official Nigeria Revenue Service channels. A business name is taxed through your State Internal Revenue Service. A company files with the NRS.

28
00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:46.820
Take your CAC documents, ID and BVN to a bank and open a business account.

29
00:03:48.070 --> 00:03:52.310
Save your documents in two digital places and one printed folder.

30
00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:58.190
Then send all business income into that account, and pay business costs from it.

31
00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:05.760
Here is a worked example. Rukayat in Ilorin gives customers only her business account number.

32
00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:11.270
In her first month, customers pay 185,000 naira.

33
00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:20.350
She pays 112,000 for flour, sugar, gas and packaging from the business account, and keeps every receipt.

34
00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:26.960
At month end she pays herself a fixed 40,000 naira, recorded as owner's drawings.

35
00:04:28.210 --> 00:04:43.400
That leaves 33,000 naira in the business. Her real profit is 185,000 minus 112,000, which is 73,000 naira, and her bank statement now supports any loan or tax filing.

36
00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:57.320
To remember. Match your NIN exactly. Fix only what a query asks. Shares divide ownership. Keep the account, email and shares in your own name. And separate business money from day one.

37
00:04:58.570 --> 00:05:06.190
Now do a dry run on the portal, draft your company decision sheet if you need one, and complete your after-registration set-up.

38
00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:08.480
Continue with the lessons below.
